From 7d421ee811081185276c163a2d9ea68bc3a470ba Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rolando Trevino <rolotrealanis@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 15:39:24 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] drm/apple: Offer a ProMotion panel's fixed refresh rates
 as modes

A ProMotion panel reports one timing: its native size at 120 Hz, with an
adaptive-sync range or with both range fields zero (the T6050 panel).
macOS offers the fixed rates 60, 59.94, 50, 48 and 47.95 Hz besides
ProMotion and 120 Hz, all on that one timing. DRM sees only the 120 Hz
mode, so a compositor cannot be asked for any of them.

Behind appledrm.promotion_rates=1, add those rates and 100 Hz to an
internal panel's catalog as modes of its preferred 120 Hz timing: the
same timing and color IDs, pixel clock and horizontal timing, with the
vertical front porch stretched so that DRM reports the rate. Rates
outside the timing's adaptive-sync range (24 Hz up to the timing's rate
when its range fields are zero), or that the firmware already has a
timing of that size for, are left out. The mode set of such a mode sends
the adaptive-sync parameter with the rate as both its minimum and its
media target rate, and the fractional-rate value for 59.94 and 47.95 Hz;
its swaps carry presentation timestamps.

The rates are opt-in until it has been measured on a panel that the
firmware holds them; the desktop 26.x mode set, which programs adaptive
sync its own way, does not get them. A refused adaptive-sync parameter
is now logged.

Assisted-by: LLM Claude
Signed-off-by: Rolando Trevino <rolotrealanis@gmail.com>
